# Evidence: Wine makes the tint2 panel segfault

## 1. What the user sees

Fresh volume, first boot: the panel is drawn, then disappears, then the whole
screen is black except the cursor. It used to stay broken until the container
was recreated.

Sampler inside the container, every 5s:

    t=0s   tint2=[40]   colours=780  wineboot=[57]   <- desktop coming up, panel painting
    t=5s   tint2=[]     colours=633  wineboot=[150]  <- panel process gone, wineboot running
    t=10s  tint2=[]     colours=2    wineboot=[]     <- black from here on
    t=125s tint2=[]     colours=2

## 2. It is a SIGSEGV, not a clean exit

supervisord.log (first seen on the deployed container, which is why it took a
supervised tint2 to notice):

    WARN exited: tint2 (terminated by SIGSEGV (core dumped); not expected)

Each restart crashed again within 4-24s: 14 crashes over the first ~2 minutes
of a fresh volume.

## 3. Ruled out

  * OOM            -- /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.events: oom_kill 0, memory.max=max
  * X screen saver -- xset q timeout stayed 600s, `xset s reset` changed nothing
  * other clients  -- openbox / Xvfb / x11vnc ran through it untouched
  * no wine        -- the v1 baseline image (no wine installed) never shows it
  * pgrep -x       -- matches nothing in this image (busybox semantics); it
                      reports tint2 dead when it is alive and vice versa. Use
                      `pgrep tint2` or `ps -eo pid,args | grep`.

## 4. gdb backtrace (the actual cause)

Ran tint2 under gdb as a supervised program in a throwaway container
(see dbg-tint2-gdb.sh):

    Thread 1 "tint2" received signal SIGSEGV, Segmentation fault.
    0x00005599f3abc213 in gradient_point_area_dependent ()
    #0 gradient_point_area_dependent
    #1 gradient_init
    #2 area_gradients_create
    #3 set_task_state
    #5 taskbar_refresh_tasklist
    #6 handle_event_property_notify
    #7 handle_x_events
    #8 run_tint2_event_loop
    #9 tint2
    #10 main

A PropertyNotify makes tint2 refresh the taskbar, and its gradient code crashes.
That is a tint2 bug; the trigger is Wine's window churn. Wine spawns a whole
process family to build a prefix or start an app -- services.exe,
winedevice.exe, plugplay.exe, explorer.exe /desktop -- and each one brings X
windows and taskbar entries up and down.

## 5. Fixes

(a) Build the Wine prefix HEADLESS: wine-prefix-init --headless. wineboot does
    not need a display (it unpacks drive_c, enumerates fonts through fontconfig
    and writes the registry), so with no DISPLAY there is no X connection, no
    window churn and no crash:

        headless=no   (DISPLAY=:1)  -> 14 crashes
        headless=yes  (DISPLAY='')  ->  0 crashes   (same 90s test container)

(b) tint2 runs under supervisor (autorestart=true). Launching a Windows app
    still costs exactly one crash -- Wine has to start that process family --
    and supervisor brings the panel back within a second (verified on the
    deployed webtop3: 1 crash, panel back, uptime kept growing).

## 6. Self-inflicted trap worth remembering

The first --headless implementation used `unset DISPLAY`, but the script then
ran `export DISPLAY="${DISPLAY-:1}"`, and that default fires only when DISPLAY
is *unset* -- so it silently put :1 back. "Headless" was a no-op and the crashes
continued (4 instead of 14). Setting an explicitly empty value is what works:

    if [ "${1:-}" = "--headless" ]; then DISPLAY=""; HEADLESS=yes; fi
    export DISPLAY="${DISPLAY-:1}"

The init log now states the mode outright:
`creating prefix /config/.wine (headless=yes DISPLAY='')`

## 7. Why "RUNNING" was not a good enough check

A crash loop samples as RUNNING roughly half the time, so "service is RUNNING"
passed while the panel was restarting every 2 seconds. scripts/verify.sh now
asserts instead:

  * zero `tint2.*SIGSEGV` in supervisord.log before any Windows app is launched
  * the same tint2 pid before and after a 12s wait (startup, and again after the
    Wine app launch)
  * the desktop still delivers >20 unique colours afterwards
